            "english": "A DataFrame is a table — made of rows and columns, just like an Excel sheet or a school register. Each column has a name, such as \"name\", \"class\" and \"marks\", and each row shows one student. Pandas is an outside package that gives you DataFrames. To install it on your own computer, write \"pip install pandas\", and then use it with \"import pandas as pd\". To make a DataFrame, write \"df = pd.DataFrame([{\"name\": \"Rahul\", \"marks\": 78}, {\"name\": \"Priya\", \"marks\": 92}])\" — this turns a list of dicts into a table. To read data from a file, write \"pd.read_csv(\"marks.csv\")\" — this turns a CSV file into a DataFrame. The strength of a DataFrame is that work happens on a whole column at once: \"df[\"marks\"].mean()\" gives the average of all marks, and \"df[df[\"marks\"] > 80]\" gives only the rows where marks are above 80. Note: Pandas is not installed in the runner on this website, so the runnable code in this article will build the same table with a list of dicts and do the same jobs.",
